    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "## Mean delayed discharge\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n## Introduction and background\n\n... To be reviewed in October 2026. Details, analyses and recommendations are provided below.\n\n## Rationale for new or revised indicator\n\nThe model does not currently identify inpatient activity occurring after a patient is ready for discharge as a distinct efficiency opportunity. The availability of Discharge Ready Date in HES provides an opportunity to identify and quantify potentially avoidable bed days between a patient becoming ready for discharge and their actual discharge, using a methodology aligned with the NHS England approach. This differs from existing approaches, which either identify activity exceeding an expected length of stay, such as HRG trim points, or use broader criteria to identify activity where there may be scope to reduce length of stay, rather than directly identifying the period following readiness for discharge. \n\nReference: NHS England, Discharge Ready Date (DRD) guidance and scenarios, updated 20 August 2026. [NHS England DRD guidance](https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/discharge-ready-date-drd-guidance-and-scenarios/)\n\n## Discharge ready date data quality\n\nFollowing guidance from the Better Care Fund for handling [discharge ready dates](https://mg.wakefield.gov.uk/documents/s136888/13d%20BCF%20Metrics%20Handbook.pdf), admissions were excluded if:\n\n- the patient was under 16 years old.\n- the length of stay was 0 days.\n- the method of admission was other than elective or emergency.\n- the patient died in hospital or was transferred to other hospitals/hospices.\n- the treatment function groups was not \"specific acute\" as defined by [NHSE in 23/24](https://www.england.nhs.uk/london/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2023/12/Specific_Acute_TFC.pdf).\n\nAfter applying these exclusions a few data quality issues were found with the discharge ready date data.\n\n### Null discharge ready dates and time series of data\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nIn 2024/25, there were \n4,433,650 \nadmissions in HES that could have a discharge ready date using the above criteria. Of these admissions, \n42.65% \nhad a discharge ready date and the remaining \n57.35% \nwere NULL.\n\n[NHS England DRD guidance](https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/discharge-ready-date-drd-guidance-and-scenarios/) requires providers to leave the discharge ready date field in data submissions as blank (NULL) when:\n\n1. the patient self-discharges against clinical advice. \n1. the patient is transferred to another provider. \n\nThe discharge ready date field can also be left blank when (3.) the date of discharge is the same. Discharge method and destination can be used to exclude cases when 1. or 2. applies, so the guidance treats the remaining NULLs as if 3. applies - the date of discharge is used in place of discharge ready date. This principle is applied to later analyses.\n\nDischarge ready dates are available from September 2023. Since, HES data covers spells up to the end of 2024/25, a new TPMA based on discharge ready dates would not have enough data for a time series by financial year, since it would only have data for 2024/25. This may affect tools like the TPMA explorer or work supporting schemes to provide TPMA estimates.\n\nAt a monthly level, the percentage of completed discharge ready dates has increased over time. Percentages of completeness are similar between HES and SUS datasets. The trend appears to continue in SUS data so we could expect similar in HES data once available.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![Percentage of admissions with completed discharge ready date over time and by data source.](mean-delayed-discharge_files/figure-html/fig-number_complete_over_time-1.png){#fig-number_complete_over_time width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Negative delays\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nIn 2024/25, there were \n4,080 \nspells \n(0.09%)\nin HES with a discharge ready date after the discharge date. Of these spells:\n\n- the discharge ready date was on average \n6.36 \ndays (standard deviation \n27.98) \nafter the discharge date.\n- 2,122 (52.01%)\nhad a discharge ready date one day after the discharge date.\n\nIt is assumed that this is due to data quality issues and so in later analyses when the discharge ready date is after the discharge date the discharge date is used instead.\n\n### Improbable discharge ready dates\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nIn 2024/25, 308 admissions had a discharge ready date recorded as \"1900-01-01\". This affects admissions over a few months:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"tabwid\"><style>.cl-c83bdc8c{}.cl-c82ec0e2{font-family:'Arial';font-size:12pt;font-weight:bold;font-style:normal;text-decoration:none;color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);background-color:transparent;}.cl-c82ec100{font-family:'Arial';font-size:12pt;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal;text-decoration:none;color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);background-color:transparent;}.cl-c834398c{margin:0;text-align:center;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);padding-bottom:2pt;padding-top:2pt;padding-left:2pt;padding-right:2pt;line-height: 1;background-color:transparent;}.cl-c83439a0{margin:0;text-align:left;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);padding-bottom:2pt;padding-top:2pt;padding-left:2pt;padding-right:2pt;line-height: 1;background-color:transparent;}.cl-c83439a1{margin:0;text-align:right;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);padding-bottom:2pt;padding-top:2pt;padding-left:2pt;padding-right:2pt;line-height: 1;background-color:transparent;}.cl-c834940e{width:1.406in;background-color:rgba(249, 191, 7, 1.00);vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c8349422{width:2.787in;background-color:rgba(249, 191, 7, 1.00);vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c8349423{width:1.406in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c834942c{width:2.787in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c834942d{width:1.406in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c834942e{width:2.787in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}</style><table data-quarto-disable-processing='true' class='cl-c83bdc8c'><thead><tr style=\"overflow-wrap:break-word;\"><th class=\"cl-c834940e\"><p class=\"cl-c834398c\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec0e2\">Month</span></p></th><th class=\"cl-c8349422\"><p class=\"cl-c834398c\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec0e2\">Number of admissions with </span><br><span class=\"cl-c82ec0e2\"> discharge ready date 1900-01-01</span></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"overflow-wrap:break-word;\"><td class=\"cl-c8349423\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a0\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">September 2024</span></p></td><td class=\"cl-c834942c\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a1\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">5</span></p></td></tr><tr style=\"overflow-wrap:break-word;\"><td class=\"cl-c8349423\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a0\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">October 2024</span></p></td><td class=\"cl-c834942c\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a1\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">244</span></p></td></tr><tr style=\"overflow-wrap:break-word;\"><td class=\"cl-c8349423\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a0\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">November 2024</span></p></td><td class=\"cl-c834942c\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a1\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">19</span></p></td></tr><tr style=\"overflow-wrap:break-word;\"><td class=\"cl-c8349423\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a0\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">December 2024</span></p></td><td class=\"cl-c834942c\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a1\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">13</span></p></td></tr><tr style=\"overflow-wrap:break-word;\"><td class=\"cl-c8349423\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a0\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">January 2025</span></p></td><td class=\"cl-c834942c\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a1\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">16</span></p></td></tr><tr style=\"overflow-wrap:break-word;\"><td class=\"cl-c834942d\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a0\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">February 2025</span></p></td><td class=\"cl-c834942e\"><p class=\"cl-c83439a1\"><span class=\"cl-c82ec100\">11</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThese improbable discharge ready dates are replaced with the discharge date.\n\n### Outliers\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nIn 2024/25, the mean duration of delay was \n0.78 \ndays (standard deviation 8.97) and 99.99% of admissions with a discharge ready date had a delay of \n120\ndays or less. There were \n455 \nadmissions with delays longer than this, ranging from \n121 \nto \n8,398 \ndays.\n\nThese longer durations of delay may be due to data quality issues and so in these cases the duration of the delay was capped at 120 days.\n\n## Proposed new or revised code definition\n\n1. Exclude admissions where: \n    - the patient was under 16 years old.\n    - the length of stay was 0 days.\n    - the method of admission was other than elective or emergency.\n    - the patient died in hospital or was transferred to other hospitals/hospices.\n    - the treatment function groups was not \"specific acute\" as defined by [NHSE in 23/24](https://www.england.nhs.uk/london/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2023/12/Specific_Acute_TFC.pdf).\n2. Derive discharge ready date by: \n    - replacing NULL discharge ready dates with the discharge date.\n    - replacing discharge ready dates that happen after the discharge date with the discharge date.\n    - replace discharge ready dates recorded as \"1900-01-01\" with the discharge date.\n3. Calculate the delay duration as the number of days between the discharge ready date and the discharge date.\n4. Cap delay durations at 120 days.\n5. Calculate the mean delay duration.\n\n## Summary of key points\n\n...\n\n## Demonstrating the scale of new TPMA indicator\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nFor the \n4,433,650 \nadmissions in 2024/25 that qualified for a delayed discharge assessment, there was a total of \n3,397,193 \ndays between the discharge ready date and the discharge date spent in hospital. So on average, each hospital admission had a delayed duration of \n0.77 \ndays. Of admissions in 2024/25, \n87.82% \nwere discharged on their discharge ready date.\n\n### Over time\n\nThe mean duration of delay has increased over the months. This may reflect the increasing completion rate of the discharge ready date column in the data since it is a similar pattern to @fig-number_complete_over_time. More time and data may be required to see if this stabilises before using the data as a baseline year in the model.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![The average duration of delay for hospital admissions by month.](mean-delayed-discharge_files/figure-html/fig-scale-1.png){#fig-scale width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## New TPMA characteristics\n\n...\n\n### By age and sex\n\nThe mean duration of delay increases with age.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![Age and sex population pyramid for the new TPMA.](mean-delayed-discharge_files/figure-html/fig-pyramid-1.png){#fig-pyramid width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### By specialty group (medical, surgical, other)\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nThe average duration of delay in 2024/25 is higher for medical specialty groups compared to surgical and other specialty groups. This is likely due to the smaller percentage of admissions discharged on the discharge ready date for medical specialties. Also, trends for average duration of delay are increasing for medical specialties.\n\n\n::: {#tbl-scale-specialty_group .cell tbl-cap='The number of admissions in 2024/25, the total delay duration and the average duration of delay by month and specialty group.'}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"tabwid\"><style>.cl-c9881f9c{}.cl-c97b0dfc{font-family:'Arial';font-size:12pt;font-weight:bold;font-style:normal;text-decoration:none;color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);background-color:transparent;}.cl-c97b0e06{font-family:'Arial';font-size:12pt;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal;text-decoration:none;color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);background-color:transparent;}.cl-c98071fc{margin:0;text-align:center;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);padding-bottom:2pt;padding-top:2pt;padding-left:2pt;padding-right:2pt;line-height: 1;background-color:transparent;}.cl-c9807206{margin:0;text-align:left;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);padding-bottom:2pt;padding-top:2pt;padding-left:2pt;padding-right:2pt;line-height: 1;background-color:transparent;}.cl-c980d0ca{width:1.424in;background-color:rgba(249, 191, 7, 1.00);vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d0de{width:1.971in;background-color:rgba(249, 191, 7, 1.00);vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d0df{width:2.314in;background-color:rgba(249, 191, 7, 1.00);vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d0e8{width:1.758in;background-color:rgba(249, 191, 7, 1.00);vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d0e9{width:0.6in;background-color:rgba(249, 191, 7, 1.00);vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d0ea{width:1.424in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d0f2{width:1.971in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d0fc{width:2.314in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d0fd{width:1.758in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d106{width:0.6in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d107{width:1.424in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rgba(102, 102, 102, 1.00);border-top: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-left: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);border-right: 0 solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.00);margin-bottom:0;margin-top:0;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;}.cl-c980d110{width:1.971in;background-color:transparent;vertical-align: middle;border-bottom: 1.5pt solid rg